# ridl-diag-showcase coverage notes

This workspace is crafted so its compile emits one instance of every diagnostic
the ridl profile implements. The `@ridl-diag-showcase` diagnostics snapshot
(`crates/ridlc/tests/snapshots/`) is the de-facto ridl diagnostic index until
the error-index website (E4.2), the way `diag-showcase` is for the typl codes.

Two members, because one package cannot provoke everything:

- `main/` — package `diag.ridl`. One file per diagnostic family, so a parse
  error in one family never suppresses the checker in another. Its manifest
  carries an invalid `[defaults].timing`, which is MANI-009; the built-in
  `[100ms..1000ms]` is the fallback, so RIDL-100 still reports concrete bounds.
- `twin/` — package `diag.twin`. It exists only to republish the service name
  `diag.showcase.duplicate`, because the service catalog is a workspace-wide
  namespace and RIDL-140 cannot be provoked from a single package.

`main/narrowing.ridl` and `main/narrowing_returns.ridl` cover what typl shapes
may cross an interaction boundary — optional in any interaction position, a map
in any position, a collection in return position. They report FORM-101 and
FORM-102, which the set-equality guard raises from several other places and so
cannot tell apart, which is why
`showcase_pins_the_interaction_boundary_narrowings` asserts each *message*
individually. The rule is inherited by E5's function signatures and E7's
registry, so a position that quietly started accepting one of these would
otherwise go unnoticed.

`main/inline_shape.ridl` is the exception to "one file per family": it re-provokes
RIDL-201, RIDL-301, RIDL-302 and RIDL-308 from inside a service's *inline* shape.
Those four are already covered from a named `interface`; provoking them a second
time through `Service.shape` is what makes inline-shape coverage structural
rather than incidental. The per-member checks are descendant-based, so they were
never at risk — but "never at risk" is a claim about today's implementation, and
the file is what keeps it true.

`main/exposure.ridl` covers the visibility boundary (issue #161): TYPL-005 from
a public `interface` over an `internal` payload, parameter, return type,
collection length bound and contract constant, and RIDL-143 from a public
`service` publishing an `internal` interface. It carries `internal interface
Backdoor` as the control — the same `internal` names in the same positions,
drawing nothing — because the rule is about a *public* item naming a
package-private one, and the failure worth guarding against in the other
direction is rejecting a correct file. The legal case is compiled rather than
only checked: `veh-cluster` holds an `internal` interface over an `internal`
payload, and its rustc proof denies `private_interfaces` by name.

The file also holds the pair that separates the two clause kinds. A `require`
sees the interface's own signals (ridl §13) and an `ensure` does not, so the
signal `HANDLE_MAX` deliberately collides with the constant of that name:
`trim`'s `require` reads the signal and exposes nothing, `drain`'s `ensure`
reads the constant and does. The first version of the #161 fix re-derived the
binding order from the syntax instead of asking the resolver, matched a signal
whatever the clause kind, and accepted the `ensure` half in silence — a defect
review caught and no single-clause fixture could have. Keep both clauses.

## Codes exercised here

- RIDL: 100, 101, 102, 103 (on an `event` and, since ADR-0015 widened the
  rule, on a `command` and a `query`), 104, 105, 106 (both remaining
  positions: `fixed` timing and the `fixed` attribute block — `command` and
  `query` left this rule when ADR-0015 admitted the range form on them), 107,
  108, 109, 110, 112 (both spellings: a bare query, and `@[20ms..]` declaring
  a throttle with no response bound), 140, 141, 143,
  201, 202, 301, 302, 303, 304, 305, 306, 307, 308, 401, 402, 403, 404, 405,
  406.
- FORM: 106, 107, 108 (the general form §4.3 attribute allow-list).
- MANI: 009.
- TYPL: 005 (the visibility rule, reaching the interaction layer), 301, 303, 304
  (the profile boundary, folded in from the E1 debt list).

The list above is not prose: `crates/ridlc/tests/corpus.rs` holds it as
`RIDL_PROFILE_CODES` and asserts the showcase emits **exactly** that set —
missing and extra codes both fail — plus a table pinning each code's severity
against the reference §16 classification.

## Codes NOT exercised here, and why

- **RIDL-407** (interaction ordinal changed against a published baseline). The
  desk check reads a workspace-local baseline directory, which is outside
  `ridlc`'s source-to-IR function (ADR-0008 decisions 9 and 13), so it is never
  a compile diagnostic. Its living example is the committed corpus member
  `crates/ridl/tests/baseline-corpus/` — a package plus a committed
  `.ridl/baseline/corpus.baseline.ir.json` — driven by
  `check_reports_ordinal_drift_against_the_committed_baseline`.
- **TYPL-302** (duration literal or timing annotation in a typl context). The
  E1 `diag-showcase` entry is already its artifact of record; repeating it here
  would duplicate an index entry rather than add one.
- The typl, FORM parse, and MANI codes E1 covers keep their home in
  `diag-showcase/NOTES`, including the ones that entry records as not
  exercisable.

## Observations recorded while building this entry

None of these is worked around in the corpus. Each is written the way it
behaves, so the snapshot pins the behaviour rather than hiding it.

- **Only the first profile violation in a file is reported — a defect to fix.**
  Recovery from RIDL-403 / TYPL-303 / TYPL-304 resynchronizes at the next typl
  or ridl top-level keyword (`is_top_level_start`), and a reserved word of
  another profile is not one. `profile_boundary.ridl` declares `view`, `model`
  and `component`; only `view` is reported, and the other two are absorbed into
  the same error node. That is why TYPL-303 needs its own file rather than a
  second line in `profile_boundary.typl`. The repair is small — add the family
  reserved words to the recovery sync set — and belongs to the parser.
- **MANI-009's span is a source file, not the manifest.** The manifest parser
  stores `[defaults].timing` unparsed, so the checker is what parses it, and a
  checker diagnostic must point somewhere in the package's own files. It lands
  on the first byte of the package's first file in load order — here
  `attrs.ridl`, which has nothing to do with timing. The behaviour is
  deliberate and documented at the emission site (a dropped manifest error
  would be worse than an unanchored one), but the rendered result reads oddly
  and is worth revisiting when manifest spans exist.
- **A rejected return type used to report the wrong thing and cascade — fixed.**
  A shape the interaction boundary refuses in *parameter* position gets a
  description-first message naming what is allowed ("command parameter must be a
  named type or a stream"); the same refusal in *return* position got
  `FORM-101: expected a return type`, which said nothing about what is allowed,
  and the parser then read the rest of the member as an attribute block, so
  `narrowing_returns.ridl` produced a FORM-102 and a FORM-106 "unknown attribute
  key `Level`" on top of it — the last of them rendered after unrelated files.
  The FORM-101 message now names the four shapes ridl §7.1 admits, and the
  offending run is consumed into an error node, so the entry is one diagnostic.
  (The TYPL-006 finding this once also pointed at — a repeated identical import
  reported as a collision — was fixed in #174; see `veh-cluster/NOTES`.)

- **RIDL-107 used to arrive with a contradicting FORM-102 — fixed.** The parser
  recognised the stray typl declaration, raised the generic "unexpected token in
  an interface body", and recovered it into an error node the checker then coded
  RIDL-107 — two diagnostics at the same span for one mistake, one of them
  saying the token was unexpected when the parser had in fact expected it well
  enough to give it a dedicated recovery. `kinds.ridl` shows the single
  diagnostic now; RIDL-107 is raised where the recognition happens.
- **RIDL-405 is emitted once per participating query, not once per error
  type.** Three queries share `SharedFault`, so the note appears three times
  with the same message. The count is the lint's design, not a defect; it is
  recorded because a reader of the index will notice the repetition.
